# Manuscript PDF page 97

                              THE LEDGER



   “Ellos cuentan,” I said finally. My voice came out strange.
   “Nosotros recordamos.” She looked at me with her whole face. “You
remember I said it.”
   “Not until the bridge.”
   “Not until the bridge,” she agreed. “Así es. It touches you, and then
you know.” She got to her feet, slowly, in sections, and went to the
closet.
   She took out the bundle in both arms the way you carry a child
that’s asleep.
   She set it on the bed among the pages and began to unwrap it. Lay‐
ers of cloth, old, soft, smelling of something sweet and burnt. The last
layer came away, and I understood why I’d been told not to touch.
   It was a stone, dark, fine-grained, about the size of two cupped
hands, polished by a great many palms. On the front, a face: wide eyes,
open mouth. On the back, carved in low relief, the hunched legs of a
woman squatting to give birth. One of the three small points at its
crown was broken and mended with cord and something that might
once have been resin. Around the base ran a band of señales, spirals
and little hooked triangles, the same hand as the coin.
   “Se llama Atabey,” said Abuela. “La Madre de las aguas.”
   The Mother of the waters.
   The coin in my pocket had gone warm the second the cloth came off.
Now it was hot. It pulled against the fabric of my jeans, a small, definite
tug toward the bed.
   “Sácala,” she said. “Pero no la toques.” Take it out. But don’t touch
her.
   I took the coin out. I held it a hand’s width above the stone.
   The sound of the house changed.
   The colmado speaker across the street had been going all morning,
the same bright merengue with the same pressed place under it every
eight seconds, and I had stopped hearing it the way you stop hearing a
refrigerator. Now it went on, and the pressed place had gone out of it.
The merengue was just a merengue. The vendor’s voice was just a
voice. The room had air in it.




